Title: User Activation Funnel Analysis (MySQL)

Business Goal
Measure conversion across the activation funnel and identify where users drop off + which segments underperform.

Dataset

5,000 users

100,000 events

Attributes: country, device, marketing_channel

Funnel events: signup → verify_email → onboarding_completed → view_feature → add_to_cart → purchase

Method (SQL Approach)

Built a per-user funnel table using MIN(CASE WHEN...)

Computed step counts + stepwise conversion rates

Segmented funnel performance by device

Measured time-to-convert using TIMESTAMPDIFF and window functions for percentiles

Key Findings

Overall conversion (signup → purchase): 7.6%

Largest drop-off step: Viewing Feature → Add to Cart (18.66% loss)

Best segment: IOS with 9.09% conversion

Time-to-purchase: Android P50 142 hours, P90 308 hours
                  iOS P50 143 hours, P90 315 hours
                  Web P50 148 hours, P90 309 hours

Recommendations

Reduce friction at the highest drop-off step (View Feature → Add to Cart)
Improve pricing clarity, strengthen primary CTAs, and simplify the path from feature discovery to cart action.

Add behavior-based nudges for slow-converting users
Trigger email/push reminders for users who view features but do not add to cart or purchase within 48–72 hours.

Replicate high-performing iOS patterns across platforms
Audit iOS onboarding and checkout flows and apply successful UX patterns to Android and Web to close the conversion gap.

Shorten time-to-purchase for long-tail users (high P90)
Introduce limited-time incentives, contextual help, or FAQs to reduce hesitation among users taking more than a week to convert.

Implement segment-specific optimization strategies
Focus on improving Android onboarding flow and evaluate traffic quality from underperforming channels to increase overall funnel efficiency.

Artifacts
schema.sql, users.sql, events.sql
